<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daudi Chwa II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Daudi Chwa II of Buganda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daudi Chwa II was kabaka of Buganda from 1897 until his death in 1939. He was installed as an infant after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a> rebelled against the British, and regents led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apolo-kagwa">Apolo Kagwa</a> governed until he came of age in 1914. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buganda-agreement">Buganda Agreement</a> of 1900 was signed in his name, and he later protested against the decline of the kingdom's autonomy and customs under colonial rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daudi-chwa-ii.jpg" alt="Daudi Chwa II" width="320" height="523" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Roscoe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daudi_Cwa_of_Buganda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
